<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Shoot</h1>
<p>Skill
</p><p>Usage:
</p>
<pre>shoot &lt;target&gt;
</pre>
<p>Example:
</p>
<pre>shoot rabbit
</pre>
<p>Fires a bow, a crossbow or a sling at someone in the room. Hold the 
weapon (see <a cmd='#'>Help Hold</a>); it takes both hands, so wield nothing else. 
Each needs its own ammunition: arrows for a bow, bolts for a crossbow, 
stones for a sling. You take them from a quiver (or a pouch, for stones) 
that you wear, or else from your inventory. A magical bow needs none: it 
draws on your mana instead. You can't shoot while fighting.
</p><p>Whether you hit depends on your skill with the weapon (bow, crossbow or 
sling) and your dexterity, against your target's dexterity and wits. A 
hit does the weapon's damage, more if the arrow or bow is made for slaying 
that kind of creature; the arrow is left in the target, or falls to the 
ground. To shoot a player, both of you must have signed the Book of 
Blood.
</p><p>You need the weapon skill (bow, crossbow or sling) to shoot at all:
</p>
<ul><li>bow: from guild level 10, the rangers, the assassins and thieves (up to 80%), the fighters (up to 70%) and the barbarians (up to 60%). Practising it needs dexterity 30.</li>
<li>crossbow: from guild level 20, the assassins and fighters (up to 90%), the rangers and knights (up to 80%) and the barbarians (up to 70%). Practising it needs dexterity 30.</li>
<li>sling: from guild level 1, the assassins, rangers and thieves, the fighters (up to 70%) and the barbarians (up to 50%); the druids from guild level 10 (up to 70%). Outside the guilds, Tue Ironside, in the back room of the inn in Udgaard, teaches it to anyone, up to 35%. Practising it needs dexterity 20.</li></ul>
<p><br />
